    "summary": "Blazing Here Comes Sunshine & marathon Truckin' → Other One jams",
    "blurb": "The first set delivers a solid run of classics highlighted by a scorching Here Comes Sunshine. The second set explodes with an extended Truckin' → The Other One sandwich that weaves through Big River, Eyes Of The World and closes with a soaring Wharf Rat coda. Keith’s piano drives the jams while Jerry’s lead work and Phil’s bass keep the momentum relentless.",
    "review": "San Diego Sports Arena on November 14‑1973 captures the band in peak late‑fall form. After a sturdy first set that includes a fiery Here Comes Sunshine, Black Throated Wind and a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider, the Dead launch into one of the most celebrated second‑set marathons of the tour. Truckin' erupts into The Other One and mutates through Big River and Eyes Of The World before bursting back into The Other One again and spilling over into an unforgettable Wharf Rat coda. Jerry Garcia’s soaring lead tones and expressive vocals (especially on Sugaree) ride atop Phil Lesh’s thunderous bass bombs, while Keith Godchaux’s monstrous piano adds both harmonic depth and melodic fire. Bill Kreutzmann’s syncopated drumming and Mickey Hart’s complementary textures keep the groove fluid, and Bob Weir supplies passionate vocal phrasing throughout the jams. The Miller soundboard capture (gd1973-11-14.sbd.miller.79049.flac16) presents this performance with crystal‑clear balance, making it the definitive listening source.",
